
  [1mOne refinement to approach 1.[0m I'd run fan-out [3minline[0m, inside that same transaction, not in the worker. Creating 20
  [38;5;153mNotification[39m rows is a single bulk insert — microseconds — and doing it transactionally means an event can never exist
  without its notifications, which removes an entire class of "notification never arrived" bug. In-app notifications
  become instant, and the only thing left needing async is email. That deletes a moving part from what I proposed
  earlier; flagging it because it's a change to the approach you approved.

  [1mEmail.[0m One management command, [38;5;153msend_notification_emails[39m, run every 5 minutes by cron or a systemd timer — no Celery,
  no Redis. It finds each user with notifications where [38;5;153memailed_at IS NULL[39m, skips any already read in-app (if you've
  seen it, you don't need the email), groups the rest into one message per user, sends, and stamps [38;5;153memailed_at[39m. Digest
  users are handled by the same command with a once-daily window.

  [1mIn-app.[0m A bell in the header polls an unread-count endpoint every 60 seconds; the inbox page lists notifications
  grouped by task with mark-read and mark-all-read. No websockets — for 50 users, polling a count is a trivially cached
  query and SSE/websockets would be the most complex thing in the codebase for no felt benefit.

  [1mTemplates.[0m Notification text renders from [38;5;153m(verb, payload)[39m at display time, not at write time — so wording fixes apply
  retroactively and each channel can format differently.
